rmc_assess = """
RELEVANT LAW FOR WEIGHING THE SIX FACTORS AND SETTING THE BAND
(England and Wales — law as at September 2026)

PART A — THE OVERALL FRAMEWORK

A1. The question the band answers
- The six factors all bear on one question: is the worker performing the services as a person in business on their own account? If yes, the engagement is not employment. If no, it points to employment: Market Investigations Ltd v Minister of Social Security [1969] 2 QB 173 (Cooke J), approved by the Privy Council in Lee Ting Sang v Chung Chi-Keung [1990] 2 AC 374.
- Cooke J's list of considerations is indicative and not exhaustive. It includes whether the individual provides their own equipment, whether they hire their own helpers, the degree of financial risk taken, the degree of responsibility for investment and management, and whether and how far they can profit from sound management. No exhaustive list can be compiled, and no single factor is decisive.
- These factors sit under the third condition in Ready Mixed Concrete (South East) Ltd v Minister of Pensions and National Insurance [1968] 2 QB 497 (MacKenna J): whether the other provisions of the contract are consistent with its being a contract of service. That condition is reached only after personal service, mutuality of obligation and control have been assessed, and those are not among the six factors banded here.
- A useful reformulation for skilled work is simply: whose business is it? Lane v Shire Roofing Co (Oxford) Ltd [1995] IRLR 493 (CA).

A2. Standing back, not adding up
- The correct approach is to paint an overall picture from the accumulated detail of the case, and to stand back and make an informed, considered qualitative appreciation of the whole. It is a matter of evaluation, not of mechanically counting factors on each side: Hall v Lorimer [1994] 1 WLR 209 (CA) (Nolan LJ, approving Mummery J at [1992] 1 WLR 939).
- The relative weight of each factor varies with the facts of the particular case. A factor that is decisive in one engagement may be marginal in another. The object of the exercise is to reach an overall impression, and the impression can be clear even where individual factors conflict (Hall v Lorimer).
- Factors should not simply be added together in the hope that the total determines the answer. A structured approach is required, in which each factor is related back to the statutory or common law question it is helping to answer: Sejpal v Rodericks Dental Ltd [2022] EAT 91.
- The breadth of the factors capable of being considered under the third limb of Ready Mixed Concrete is wide, and the overall assessment is an evaluative finding of fact and degree: HMRC v Professional Game Match Officials Ltd [2024] UKSC 29. In that case the Supreme Court remitted the multi-factorial assessment rather than substituting its own view, which reflects how fact-sensitive this stage is.
- HMRC v Atholl House Productions Ltd [2022] EWCA Civ 501 confirms a final stage of standing back and asking whether the individual was in business on their own account, having regard where appropriate to the individual's wider career and business activities, so far as those would have been known to both parties.

PART B — THE MORE PROBATIVE FACTORS

B1. In business on own account
- This is the central question itself, not merely an indicator, which is why it carries the greatest weight (Market Investigations; Lee Ting Sang; Hall v Lorimer).
- Indicators that the worker is in business on their own account:
  - a genuine business infrastructure: own business name and branding, premises, staff or subcontractors, business insurance, accountancy and marketing costs
  - actively marketing services to the world at large, rather than being recruited to work for one principal as an integral part of its operations: Cotswold Developments Construction Ltd v Williams [2006] IRLR 181 (Langstaff J)
  - a portfolio of clients over time, with the ability to take on more work and turn work away
  - responsibility for investment and management, and the ability to profit from sound management (Market Investigations)
  - the worker holds themselves out as, and behaves as, an independent supplier
- Indicators against:
  - no investment, no management responsibility, no ability to profit from managing the work well: Lee Ting Sang [1990] 2 AC 374, where a mason working for a subcontractor was an employee on exactly this reasoning
  - complete economic dependence on a single engager over a sustained period
  - the worker is doing the client's business, not their own
- Important qualifications:
  - Running a genuine business elsewhere does not automatically defeat employment or worker status for a particular engagement. The question is the character of this engagement: Hospital Medical Group Ltd v Westwood [2012] EWCA Civ 1005, where a surgeon with his own practice was still a worker for the clinic.
  - A business need not involve capital investment. A skilled person may run a genuine business on skill alone (Hall v Lorimer).
  - Contracting through a personal service company does not by itself show the worker is in business on their own account, particularly where the intermediary was imposed by the engager and the worker had little to do with its operation: Lutz v Ryanair DAC and Storm Global Ltd (CA, July 2025).

B2. Financial risk
- Financial risk goes directly to the Market Investigations question and was decisive in Ready Mixed Concrete itself. The driver bought and maintained his lorry, bore the running costs, could increase his profit by efficient operation and was exposed to loss. The chance of profit and the risk of loss in the business of carriage were his, so the contract was one of carriage and not of service, despite substantial control by the company.
- The relevant risk is entrepreneurial risk: the capacity to make a real loss on the engagement, not merely to earn less.
  - The risk of not being offered work, or of earning less by working fewer hours, is not entrepreneurial risk: Lee Ting Sang; Uber BV v Aslam [2021] UKSC 5. In Uber, drivers bore vehicle and fuel costs but Uber fixed the fares, so the only route to higher earnings was longer hours. That is not the risk of a person in business.
- Forms of genuine financial risk:
  - fixed-price or quoted work where overruns in time or cost fall on the worker
  - significant unreimbursed costs: materials, plant, premises, staff, subcontractors, insurance
  - bad debt risk and the risk of non-payment
  - paying fees to the engager or venue that must be covered before any profit: Stringfellow Restaurants Ltd v Quashie [2012] EWCA Civ 1735, where the dancer could end a night out of pocket and the economic risk was hers
  - rectification risk: having to put defective or unsatisfactory work right in the worker's own time and at their own expense, without further payment. This is a well-established indicator of self-employment, principally through HMRC guidance (Employment Status Manual, ESM0500 series) and the CEST tool rather than leading appellate authority, and falls within the "degree of financial risk" limb of Market Investigations
  - carrying professional indemnity or public liability insurance, and giving warranties or guarantees on the work
- Weight qualifications:
  - Risk allocated on paper but never borne in practice carries little weight: Autoclenz Ltd v Belcher [2011] UKSC 41.
  - A trivial rectification cost, or work where defects are practically impossible, is a weak indicator.
  - Some financial exposure is compatible with worker status where the individual is integrated and subordinate: Pimlico Plumbers Ltd v Smith [2018] UKSC 29, where the plumber supplied his own van, tools and materials and was still a worker.
  - Bearing costs is not the same as bearing risk. Reimbursed expenses are neutral.

PART C — THE MODERATELY PROBATIVE FACTOR

C1. Integration
- A person is employed where the work is done as an integral part of the business, and is a contractor where the work, though done for the business, is not integrated into it but is only accessory to it: Stevenson, Jordan & Harrison Ltd v Macdonald & Evans [1952] 1 TLR 101 (Denning LJ); see also Bank voor Handel en Scheepvaart NV v Slatford [1953] 1 QB 248 ("part and parcel of the organisation").
- Integration is strong evidence in either direction but is not conclusive. MacKenna J in Ready Mixed Concrete doubted the test's precision and declined to adopt it as a condition. It operates as one of the "other provisions" under condition (iii).
- Indicators of integration:
  - presented to customers, suppliers or the public as the engager's own personnel: uniform, branded vehicle, identity card, company email, job title, listing on the website or staff directory (Pimlico Plumbers [2018] UKSC 29; Whittaker v Minister of Pensions and National Insurance [1967] 1 QB 156)
  - the engager owns the customer relationship, fixes the price and prevents the worker building their own client base (Uber; Pimlico)
  - receipt of staff benefits: contractual holiday or sick pay, pension, bonus, staff discounts and facilities
  - employer-funded general training and career development, as distinct from short induction, safety or system-access training
  - inclusion in appraisal, disciplinary, grievance and other HR processes; line management of the engager's staff
  - doing core, continuing work alongside employees doing the same job, and taking on other duties when asked (Whittaker)
  - integration with directly employed staff through rosters, bases, assessments and performance reviews did not prevent, and supported, a finding of worker status in Lutz (CA, July 2025)
- Indicators against integration:
  - delivering a discrete project or specialist service accessory to the business (Stevenson, Jordan)
  - short engagements across many clients: Hall v Lorimer, where temporary integration into each production did not make the vision mixer part of any one organisation; Addison v London Philharmonic Orchestra Ltd [1981] ICR 261, where freelance musicians were integral to the orchestra when playing but were not employees
- Weight qualifications:
  - Integration cannot create employment where the irreducible minimum is absent: O'Kelly v Trusthouse Forte plc [1984] QB 90; Stringfellow v Quashie. Those elements sit outside these six factors, so integration here should be read as evidence of the character of the engagement rather than as a substitute for them.
  - Uniforms, passes and inductions required for safety, security or regulatory reasons, which would apply to any contractor on site, are largely neutral.
  - The absence of staff benefits is usually weak evidence, because it typically reflects the label the parties adopted rather than the substance of the relationship.

PART D — THE SUPPORTING FACTORS

D1. Equipment
- Provision of one's own equipment is listed in Market Investigations and was significant in Ready Mixed Concrete, where the driver's ownership of the lorry, acquired on hire purchase and maintained at his own expense, was central.
- Weight depends on scale and on trade norms:
  - substantial plant, vehicles, machinery or systems, bought and maintained at the worker's own cost, are a real indicator of self-employment, because they combine investment with risk
  - hand tools and small items that employees in the same trade commonly supply carry little weight (Hall v Lorimer; Lee Ting Sang)
  - equipment supplied by the engager, or supplied by the worker but reimbursed, points towards employment or is neutral
- The absence of significant equipment does not defeat self-employment where the business is founded on skill rather than capital (Hall v Lorimer). This is why equipment is a supporting rather than a probative factor.

D2. Payment basis
- Payment for time (hourly, daily, weekly, monthly), regardless of outcome, points towards employment. Payment of a fixed price for a completed result, with overruns absorbed by the worker, points towards self-employment.
- Payment by output is not decisive. Homeworkers paid per item were held to be employees: Airfix Footwear Ltd v Cope [1978] ICR 1210; Nethermere (St Neots) Ltd v Gardiner [1984] ICR 612.
- Who sets the price matters. Where the engager fixes the rate and the worker cannot negotiate or price their own services, the scope for entrepreneurial profit is limited (Uber).
- Gross payment, invoicing and self-assessment treatment are labels and carry little independent weight: Ferguson v John Dawson & Partners (Contractors) Ltd [1976] 1 WLR 1213; Young & Woods Ltd v West [1980] IRLR 201.
- Payment through the engager's payroll, with PAYE and payslips, points towards employment but is not conclusive (O'Kelly).

D3. Exclusivity and duration
- Exclusivity and economic dependence on a single engager point towards employment. Freedom to work for others, and actually doing so, points towards self-employment (Hall v Lorimer, where the vision mixer worked for around twenty production companies).
- A restriction on working for competitors, or restrictive covenants limiting work for the engager's customers after the engagement, points towards employment or worker status (Pimlico Plumbers).
- Duration and continuity:
  - long, continuous, open-ended engagement points towards employment
  - short, project-based or intermittent engagements point away from it
  - but length alone is not determinative. A long fixed-term supply can still be temporary agency work, and a five-year assignment did not prevent a finding that the worker was assigned "temporarily" for the purposes of the Agency Workers Regulations 2010 (Lutz, CA, July 2025)
- Non-exclusivity is weak evidence on its own. Many employees are free to take second jobs, and a right to work elsewhere that is never exercised, or is exercised only marginally, carries little weight.
- Where the worker does not market their services to others and is recruited to work as an integral part of one principal's operations, exclusivity reinforces the integration finding (Cotswold; Westwood).

PART E — WEIGHING AND CALIBRATION

E1. Strength over count
- The exercise is qualitative. Two strong findings on the more probative factors will ordinarily outweigh a larger number of weak findings on supporting factors, because the weight of a factor depends on how directly it answers the Market Investigations question and how firmly it is established on the facts (Hall v Lorimer).
- A picture assembled from weak indicators is a weak picture, whatever the count. It should be described as such rather than converted into confidence it does not warrant.
- Where the more probative factors conflict with each other, or where they are weak and only the supporting factors are strong, the honest reading is usually a mixed one. Borderline is a legitimate outcome, not an evasion. Courts have repeatedly acknowledged that status cases can be finely balanced, and the parties' own view of the relationship may tip the balance only where the position remains genuinely ambiguous after everything else has been weighed: Massey v Crown Life Insurance Co [1978] 1 WLR 676.

E2. Substance over form
- Findings should rest on what actually happens, not on what the documents say. Written terms are disregarded where they do not reflect the true agreement, having regard to the relative bargaining power of the parties, the whole context and the parties' conduct: Autoclenz Ltd v Belcher [2011] UKSC 41; Consistent Group Ltd v Kalwak [2008] EWCA Civ 430.
- For statutory worker and employee status the question is one of statutory interpretation, and the starting point is the protective purpose of the legislation, not the contract: Uber BV v Aslam [2021] UKSC 5; applied in Martin Groom v Maritime and Coastguard Agency [2026] EWCA Civ 6 and in Lutz (CA, July 2025).
- Terms that allocate risk, deny benefits or assert independence, but that neither party ever treated as real, should be given little or no weight in the band.

E3. Cautions on the banding decision
- Do not treat the absence of staff benefits, gross payment, self-assessment treatment or a contractual "self-employed" label as strong evidence of self-employment. These are labels or consequences of labels.
- Do not treat the risk of fewer hours or of losing the engagement as financial risk (Lee Ting Sang; Uber).
- Do not treat small tools, or a site pass and induction, as meaningful indicators either way.
- Do not upgrade a factor's weight because it is the only one pointing that way. Weight comes from probative value and evidential strength, not from position in the overall split.
- Do not let a single supporting factor carry an outer band on its own. The outer bands describe a coherent picture in which the central question is answered clearly and nothing substantial pulls the other way.
- This assessment concerns the character of the engagement only. It does not determine status by itself, because personal service, mutuality of obligation and control are assessed separately, and the absence of any of those is fatal to employment regardless of how these six factors fall: Carmichael v National Power plc [1999] 1 WLR 2042; Montgomery v Johnson Underwood Ltd [2001] EWCA Civ 318.
"""